1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,540
Welcome to the QDIKW demonstrator.

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:09,060
In consulting slides, the data,
information, knowledge, wisdom pyramid is

3
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,600
often presented like magic.

4
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:15,540
Pour raw data into a graph and executive
wisdom comes out the other side.

5
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:21,700
That illusion makes it look as if a single
all-knowing mega brain is doing all the work.

6
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:25,920
And if such a mega brain existed, we
wouldn't need a knowledge management

7
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:26,680
system at all.

8
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:32,240
In real systems engineering, there is no
magic. There is only the law of

9
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:34,180
conservation of engineering effort.

10
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,960
A knowledge system cannot invent physics
out of thin air.

11
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:41,400
Its true power is separating human roles.

12
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:47,260
Today, various specialists already do this
hard work, but their outputs are trapped

13
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:51,340
in disconnected Word documents, CAD files,
and Excel spreadsheets.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,940
Let's walk through the five stages from
question to wisdom and see not just what

15
00:00:56,940 --> 00:01:01,280
happens on screen, but who had to do what
beforehand to make it work.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,860
Stage one: the question.

17
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:11,200
A fleet operator asks a seemingly simple
question. "A crack was just reported on

18
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:16,100
vehicle one. Is it safe to dispatch or is
there a risk of structural failure?"

19
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,280
Here we hit the first expectation trap.

20
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:23,320
One may assume the system will magically
spot any flaw.

21
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:28,400
In reality, the system can only answer
questions if two people laid the

22
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:31,380
groundwork long before vehicle one was
even built.

23
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:38,780
First, the IT and database architect at
the SMS and SMIM layers had to program the

24
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:44,200
graph database to support temporal
states, hierarchical paths, and rule execution.

25
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:52,240
Second, the ontology modeler at the SMRM
layer had to define the controlled vocabulary,

26
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:59,580
creating explicit classes for vehicles,
slots, parts, defects, load types, and hazards.

27
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:06,340
You cannot ask a question if the database
lacks the grammar to express it.

28
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:10,320
Stage two: data.

29
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:16,040
A maintenance technician in the workshop
inspects the vehicle.

30
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:21,160
Notice that the defect does not magically
attach itself to a component.

31
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:25,760
The technician scans the barcode of a
specific physical part,

32
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:31,840
serial number ARM_SN_404, and logs

33
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:37,740
DEFECT_742, selecting crack and typing 15
millimeters.

34
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:42,460
Why is this still just raw data?

35
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:47,740
Because the technician isn't typing
freeform prose. They are constrained by

36
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:51,840
the dropdown menus that the database
programmer and ontologist built for them.

37
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:57,300
Yet, at this level, the record is just an
isolated observation on a serial number.

38
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,940
The technician doesn't know fracture
mechanics, and the raw log doesn't know

39
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:06,720
what role ARM_SN_404 plays in the vehicle.

40
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:14,300
Stage three: information, 
giving data structural context.

41
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:20,480
This brings us to the most critical
question in engineering data management.

42
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:25,320
How does the system know that this part is
under dynamic cyclic loading?

43
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:31,280
If we forced the 3D CAD designer who
models a suspension arm in a CAD tool to

44
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:36,320
manually fill in 40 ontological attributes
about cyclic loads and vehicle-level

45
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:39,160
hazards, the project would fail within a
week.

46
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:44,140
Component designers shouldn't have to
guess system-level physics, and a spare

47
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:49,500
part sitting on a shelf in warehouse_1
experiences zero cyclic load anyway.

48
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:55,240
Instead, look at how IEC 63339 separates
their work.

49
00:03:57,260 --> 00:04:04,220
The 3D CAD designer simply registers
ARM_SN_404 and places it

50
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:06,420
into the assembly tree. Nothing more.

51
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:14,420
Meanwhile, the systems architect, or an
automated import from a DFMEA safety

52
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,560
table, defines the functional architecture
once.

53
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:24,680
They mark the functional slot,
SLOT_FRONT_SUSPENSION, as experiencing

54
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:30,200
DYNAMIC_CYCLIC loading and serving the
STEERING_CONTROL function.

55
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:37,880
The moment ARM_SN_404 is mounted into

56
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:44,600
SLOT_FRONT_SUSPENSION, the graph
automatically inherits that operational context.

57
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:49,960
The part now carries dynamic cyclic
loading, not because someone hardcoded it

58
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:53,880
onto the metal, but because of where it
currently sits in the architecture.

59
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:58,640
Stage four: knowledge.

60
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:05,320
How does the system connect a
15-millimeter crack to a catastrophic failure?

61
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:10,020
Here, a materials and fatigue specialist
steps in.

62
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:15,060
This expert knows nothing about Cypher
queries or RDF triples.

63
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:20,040
Working purely with the pre-approved
building blocks in the domain dictionary,

64
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:22,600
they formulate modular atomic rules.

65
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:25,880
Rule one: context inheritance.

66
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:31,860
Any part mounted in a slot with
DYNAMIC_CYCLIC regime is subject to

67
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:34,660
ACTIVE_CYCLIC_STRESS.

68
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:38,280
Rule two: material physics.

69
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:45,720
A crack exceeding 10 millimeters on a part
under ACTIVE_CYCLIC_STRESS triggers

70
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:48,740
RAPID_FRACTURE_PROPAGATION.

71
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:52,720
Rule three: functional safety.

72
00:05:53,460 --> 00:06:00,240
RAPID_FRACTURE_PROPAGATION in any slot
supporting STEERING_CONTROL

73
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:01,640
causes a system hazard,

74
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:05,000
LOSS_OF_CONTROL.

75
00:06:06,888 --> 00:06:07,948
Notice the boundary.

76
00:06:08,568 --> 00:06:13,328
The materials expert couldn't invent new
database structures. They could only wire

77
00:06:13,328 --> 00:06:17,548
together concepts that the IT architect
and ontologist made available.

78
00:06:18,288 --> 00:06:22,328
Because the graph engine supports
transitive inference, these three

79
00:06:22,328 --> 00:06:27,368
independent rules snap together like Lego
bricks, automatically building a causal

80
00:06:27,368 --> 00:06:31,268
bridge from the technician's barcode scan
to a vehicle-level failure.

81
00:06:33,668 --> 00:06:36,388
Finally, stage five, wisdom.

82
00:06:37,128 --> 00:06:40,028
Turning inferred knowledge into an
actionable decision.

83
00:06:42,088 --> 00:06:46,928
Traversing the causal chain, the system
tells the fleet operator, "Critical

84
00:06:46,928 --> 00:06:51,948
hazard. Loss of control risk for ground
vehicle one. Generate work order for

85
00:06:51,948 --> 00:06:53,448
suspension arm replacement."

86
00:06:55,668 --> 00:06:59,068
But what happens when reality steps
outside these rules?

87
00:07:00,868 --> 00:07:02,848
Let's test the system's blind spot.

88
00:07:03,588 --> 00:07:07,588
Suppose the technician logs a second
observation on the exact same part,

89
00:07:08,208 --> 00:07:14,428
defect_743, surface_corrosion, five square
centimeters.

90
00:07:15,368 --> 00:07:20,188
Watch the graph. The corrosion record
passes through data and attaches to the

91
00:07:20,188 --> 00:07:25,408
suspension slot as information, 
and then it stops. Why?

92
00:07:26,088 --> 00:07:30,268
Because the materials expert hasn't
authored a rule linking surface corrosion

93
00:07:30,268 --> 00:07:31,768
area to cyclic fracture,

94
00:07:32,488 --> 00:07:36,408
or perhaps the database lacks the
mathematical operators to calculate

95
00:07:36,408 --> 00:07:37,848
cross-sectional rust loss.

96
00:07:38,668 --> 00:07:43,648
Instead of hallucinating or silently
hiding the problem, a properly architected

97
00:07:43,648 --> 00:07:47,348
system explicitly flags an unmapped
engineering condition.

98
00:07:48,008 --> 00:07:50,728
Automated inference stopped at the
information layer.

99
00:07:51,348 --> 00:07:55,968
Escalate defect_743 to a human materials
specialist.

100
00:07:59,068 --> 00:08:03,688
That is the real takeaway of this
workbench. A knowledge graph is not a

101
00:08:03,688 --> 00:08:09,168
crystal ball. Without it, five specialists
still do all this work in isolated

102
00:08:09,168 --> 00:08:12,648
documents, and it takes days of meetings
to connect the dots.

103
00:08:13,328 --> 00:08:18,388
With it, each role contributes their piece
of the puzzle once within clear technical

104
00:08:18,388 --> 00:08:23,128
boundaries, allowing the system to
connect those dots in milliseconds while

105
00:08:23,128 --> 00:08:26,608
honestly showing where human engineering
judgment must take over.
